cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Smallest witnesses by girth for rad(n) | f(n)"
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
type: dataset
authors:
  - family-names: "Ellena Godoy"
    given-names: "Jorge"
abstract: >-
  For a multiplicative function f, let S(f) be the set of integers n with
  rad(n) dividing f(n), and let the covering digraph of n have the primes
  dividing n as vertices with an edge q -> p whenever p divides f(q^e), where
  q^e is the exact power of q in n. This work computes the smallest member of
  S(f) of each girth, for fifteen multiplicative functions. Any minimal witness of
  girth k has exactly k distinct prime factors and a covering digraph that is a
  pure directed cycle; its largest prime is bounded in terms of any known
  witness, which makes the enumeration finite; a per-arc strengthening of that
  bound makes the search several times cheaper; and a floor that mentions no
  witness at all, combined with doubling over an exhaustive search, removes the
  need for a known witness entirely. The bound on the largest prime requires no
  closed form and holds for every multiplicative function, and a second bound on
  the covering cost, obtained by sieving rather than by formula, is about ten
  times stronger while returning identical values. A surgery theorem inserts one
  vertex into the cycle of a witness of girth k to obtain a witness of girth k+1,
  which bounds the next smallest witness from above and, when the inserted
  stretch costs less than the exponent it saves, certifies that the next smallest
  witness is strictly smaller without computing it. All sixty-nine published
  values are proved minimal rather than minimal among the primes examined, for
  sigma, sigma*, phi*, sigma** (the sum of the biunitary divisors) and eleven
  members of the families sigma_s, sigma*_s and phi*_s; fifty-five of them had
  not been computed before. All results are verified by independent
  implementations, by brute force sharing no code with the search, against the
  OEIS entry of each function, and against the count of prime-abundant numbers
  published by Pollack and Pomerance (2012).
keywords:
  - number theory
  - arithmetic functions
  - sum of divisors
  - unitary divisors
  - prime-abundant numbers
  - covering digraph
  - girth
  - integer sequences
  - biunitary divisors
  - non-monotonicity
